362 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 780 0 R
/E (The wind tunnel was designed using a quasi-1D approach, linking area changes to target flow conditions and facility constraints. The contraction and diffuser were sized using established aerodynamic guidelines to manage flow quality and pressure recovery. A smooth wall contour was generated in Python via a cubic half-height function and iterated until the layout fit within the lab envelope. The final geometry was then validated with 2D CFD to verify the velocity field and check for separation or nonuniformity.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [36]
>>
endobj
363 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 781 0 R
/E (Figure 1 shows the overall tunnel layout from the fan through the contraction, test section, and diffuser. Figure 2 shows the Python-generated contour, highlighting the contraction into the constant-area test section and the subsequent diffuser expansion. Together, they illustrate a complete flow path designed for uniform test section flow with manageable pressure losses and a compact footprint.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [37]
>>
endobj
364 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 162 0 R
/E (Figure 3. Python Assessment )
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [38]
